SM(521,{"n":"Romans 12","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 12.mp3","d":2743.5,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.28,"So we'll go ahead and get started when we get started here."],[24.64,"In just a minute, we'll be picking up Romans chapter 12, verse 1."],[30.0,"Catch up will be quicker this time because last time we ended at a major transition point in St."],[37.52,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans."],[39.36,"As I mentioned way back in the long-ago time, the introduction to Romans and to St."],[45.04,"Paul's Epistles in general, he structures all of his epistles sort of in two major parts."],[52.56,"And the transition between those two parts is usually marked by in English the word therefore."],[59.72,"In the first part of his epistle, he'll lay out theological groundwork, do a lot of work with the Old Testament, sort of set out, set the basis for what he has to say, then he'll say therefore and move into a section of sort of more practical application of those things that he's just talked about."],[81.4,"And the therefore serves to number one mark that transition that now we're moving to a different part, but also to connect that second part to the first part."],[90.76,"So the basis for the second part is the first part."],[97.6,"Would it be fair to say that he's probably chosen the theological themes in the first part in light of what he tends to address?"],[106.94,"Exactly. Exactly. Exactly."],[109.82,"And so in the particular case of Romans, we've seen that in the first part he was talking about the relationship between Jewish believers and Gentile believers in the church, dealing with the issues created by Claudius having expelled the Jews from Rome and now their return."],[131.58,"And so he's been dealing with that at sort of a theological level."],[135.18,"He's focused a lot on the patriarchal narratives in Genesis, on Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Esau, in particular, Ishmael, and shown how there's a clear pattern in how God. has been always been dealing with his people"],[152.66,"And that that pattern continues into the new covenant."],[157.38,"It's fulfilled in Christ."],[158.9,"And so it's the same pattern but writ larger."],[161.78,"And now including not only the nation of, well, not a nation, right now a province, but including not only Judea, but all of the nations in that one plan and that one pattern."],[176.5,"And so now, based on having laid that out at the theological level, he's going to proceed here in the final chapters of his epistle to the Romans to say, okay, here's what this means for you in your Christian community in Rome, and how how you live, how you're going to live together, worship together, and be a community together in light of your shared uh confession of Christ."],[208.78,"So, unless there are any questions or uh comments or uh end notes or footnotes, uh we'll go ahead and get started in uh chapter 12, verse 1."],[222.46,"It says, I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice, wholly acceptable to God, which is your reasonable service."],[233.98,"This verse is in very King Jamesy."],[238.86,"I know this this is is. the new King James, but still very King Jamesy English"],[243.54,"Um you'll notice the therefore there in the translation has gotten moved in terms of the word order."],[250.5,"But that is the therefore."],[251.7,"So you've got I beseech you, therefore, brethren."],[254.42,"So based on everything I've just said, I beseech you by the mercies of God that you present your bodies as living sacrifices, wholly acceptable to God, which is your reasonable service."],[267.46,"Reasonable service is probably the uh oddest translation there."],[276.66,"Uh it means more than logical"],[281.39,"And then goes on, and I'll let you explain this a little bit."],[286.2,"Um, what he's urging, first of all, he says, uh present your bodies a living sacrifice."],[297.4,"Notice here he uses the word body."],[299.24,"We talked before about we have to we have to be careful when we read St."],[303.16,"Paul to pay attention to his word choice, just like the Jews and Israel are not the same thing in St."],[309.88,"Paul. We talked before about how flesh and body are not the same thing."],[314.76,"That when he talks about flesh, most of the time he's referring to sort of the sinful aspect of our now mortal human nature."],[325.72,"When he talks about our body, he's usually talking about our physicality."],[330.74,"Our physicality and our life in the material world."],[335.94,"So it's it's a bit more than literally our body."],[341.38,"It's it's uh it includes the material side of our existence."],[346.76,"Our interaction with matter."],[349.45,"Um and that for St."],[352.57,"Paul is not negative."],[354.75,"He speaks of the flesh in negative terms."],[358.23,"The body, he does not speak of in negative terms per se."],[362.23,"And consequently thought, as my parents did, that St"],[368.55,"Paul was down on the body."],[368.95,"And that's that's sort of the Gnostic reading of St"],[375.05,"Paul fails to take that distinction into account."],[378.99,"So when he says the flesh, he means meat, and therefore there's this severed distinction."],[387.39,"You have your soul, you had your rational part, which is good, and then you have the physical and material part that is bad."],[394.05,"In a Gnostic system, uh is one of the basic features of all the Gnostic systems."],[399.33,"And that's not the case for St."],[400.61,"Paul. St. Paul makes a distinction there."],[403.09,"Uh this will become even more clear when we get into First Corinthians, and he starts talking about the difference between our body now and our body in the resurrection, which is the same body. but the way in which it is transformed, the way in which it changes"],[418.41,"Namely, that what he calls the flesh goes away."],[421.85,"But our physicality does not."],[426.97,"But so here he's talking about presenting our bodies, our physicality, our life in the world as a living sacrifice."],[435.55,"He's not urging suicide or human sacrifice."],[440.48,"But living sacrifice doesn't just, I mean, that you would think would be fairly obvious."],[446.0,"But living sacrifice also means that this is ongoing."],[451.52,"This isn't a one time act."],[453.92,"When you offer a sacrifice, that is by its nature a one time act, whether it's an animal or not."],[456.48,"a Whatever food items it is, you offer as sacrifice, they're consumed, that's it."],[466.42,"So a living sacrifice implies that this is going to be an ongoing offering of our material, of our material life to God."],[476.18,"This might strike a chord with you for something we repeat in every litany, at the end of every litany in the church, uh, which is after we' cvealled to remembrance the Theotokos and all the saints, we say, Let us commend ourselves and each other and our whole life unto Christ our God."],[497.3,"Uh, this idea of offering everything."],[498.58,"offering everything And again, this is."],[500.74,"This is not St"],[505.18,"Paul saying, I urge you to go and take 10% of your material life and well-being and offer that to God."],[513.9,"He's saying your whole entire life and self and life in this world."],[520.46,"Now, in order for something to be an acceptable sacrifice to God, what is offered to God has to be holy."],[530.91,"It has to be pu.re"],[533.49,"In in the the old testament uh regulations, you offer an animal that's without blemishes, can't have a broken leg, can't have, you take your your best and you offer to God."],[543.75,"So that means that if we' ifre we're going to do this, going to on an ongoing basis offer ourselves and our lives in this world to God, we have to keep ourselves and our lives in this world holy and pure, so there'll be an acceptable sacrifice."],[562.27,"These two ideas go together."],[566.75,"Uh I remember the book of common prayer, or this phrase is a full."],[581.23,"It just says then it's translated here, your reasonable service."],[586.48,"Uh you could translate it rational worship."],[589.64,"But the idea here is that what he's done here is he's also brought in the immaterial part of ourselves."],[598.5,"He's brought into this our soul, our mind."],[603.36,"The rest of ourselves."],[604.8,"This is how, this is how we worship God."],[609.54,"With our minds and our souls, not apart from our bodies."],[614.1,"And, you know, again, we could talk about Gnosticism, but there's a certain level of, if we want to call it Gnosticism, there's a level of this Gnosticism, you know, that I think exists for all of us, at least in this day and age in the Western world, with the separation of church and state, and these kind of ideas where we don't see a direct connection."],[632.94,"Worship is something we do on occasions."],[637.03,"And it's disconnected from sort of the rest of our life and our existence."],[643.35,"And what St."],[644.23,"Paul is doing here is tying those together and saying they're inseparable."],[647.91,"They're inseparable. Offering the Eucharist, uh, celebrating the liturgy, celebrating another service."],[664.69,"But for you to worship God, you have to be doing it with your whole life at every point."],[675.75,"Again, it's not about offering part."],[679.19,"It's about offering the whole thing."],[683.83,"So in the ancient world, where what St."],[687.51,"Paul is addressing is this idea that, well, we worship God with our rational part, with our soul, with our mind, you know, and that's separated somehow from this day to day sort of thing."],[701.27,"Yeah. But, and for the Romans, that was on point at that time."],[705.91,"For us, that expresses itself more in the way in which we've segmated, segmented our lives into these different parts."],[713.51,"Well, I I think growing up and being raised Presbyterian, the strongest message I got was that religion was about getting rid of the body, physicality itself."],[729.73,"And turning your back on that and living totally an immaterial life."],[737.09,"This was very much my my mother's personal emphasis as well."],[742.19,"The morality is about abstinence from various worldly things."],[745.63,"Yeah, yeah. Yeah."],[748.35,"And uh it's uh and you know the the th always emphasizing, you know, the body dies, it's not important, um, the soul lives."],[759.79,"Uh I was raised agnostic."],[763.87,"Um well and and you know, even while most of our our Protestant friends would say would want to say that they don't hold to that."],[775.96,"Even a lot of the worship is framed in that direction."],[780.36,"Yeah. You have an iconoclastic sense, you have a very simple and plain space that's designed to direct all of your attention in one place as you sit quietly and listen."],[792.16,"And sort of intake intellectually information it's you know and it's it's highly verbal."],[800.4,"Yeah. And so there is this there is this segmentation."],[807.3,"There is this segmentation and so it should be noted there that off offering your life and your body or yourself as a living sacrifice is an active thing also it's not just a passive thing of abstaining from there are these certain worldly things that I'm not going to do in order to keep myself pure but it's an active thing."],[827.58,"Yeah in talking about Calvinism in my classes I've talked about um made up the term have no idea if it's a good one um uh negative asceticism asceticism in the sense that you don't do this and you don't do that, you don't do the other."],[844.88,"And then you know anything positive that you do."],[848.65,"Yeah. It's not about it's not about how much you pray for, example."],[852.89,"It's about how much you don't drink, dance, play cards, etc."],[857.83,"And so the idea of giving your time, giving your material wealth, giving your attention to other people who are in need, and that being a sacrifice being offered to God has has in a lot of cases sort of fallen out of the out of the picture."],[876.71,"Because and and it's largely out of a fear that if you allow for that idea, you'll create some kind of works righteousness where people think they're earning their salvation."],[887.99,"Yeah. Which, of course, isn't the case."],[891.29,"The church condemned Pelagius a long time before John Calvin lived."],[895.21,"It's just as dangerous"],[898.61,"The arrogance of I'm I'm really holy is just as inherent in all the things I don't do."],[904.85,"Yes. Yeah, you could be just as much of a Pharisee by bragging about what you don't do as bragging about what you do."],[911.09,"Um. So it's not effective."],[918.77,"And so St."],[919.89,"Paul then goes on, he's still now talking about the mind."],[923.73,"Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, that you may prove what is that good and acceptable and perfect will of God."],[936.77,"It's important that he's talking about the mind here when he talks about being conformed to this world."],[943.93,"He's not here, he's already talked about behavior."],[947.05,"He covered behavior with the living sacrifice idea and uh maintaining holiness in order to be acceptable to God."],[955.21,"Now he's talking about the mind and the way in which the mind thinks and the mind values things."],[964.09,"That's the word prove there is functioning in that way."],[967.13,"So this is prove in the sense of testing."],[973.39,"Yeah. Not like you."],[977.19,"It's used in chemistry, I think."],[979.95,"You prove a metal or something like that."],[982.11,"You prove that it's pure or something like that."],[984.01,"And that's so that you'll be able to discern what it is."],[988.54,"And we tend to talk about discerning the will of God, especially in American Christian circles, in a different way than St."],[996.38,"Paul is talking about it here."],[998.22,"A lot of times we talk about discerning the will of God in the sense that I'm trying to figure out if I should marry this person."],[1005.18,"Or I'm trying to figure out what to major in in college, or I'm trying to figure out whether to take this job or not."],[1011.26,"And we we operate under the assumption that God has already made this choice for us."],[1018.06,"And if we make the wrong choice, there's gonna be all kinds of disaster."],[1024.08,"So we have to somehow figure out what we have to somehow voodoo what it is that God wants us to do."],[1029.12,"Uh-huh And if we can't figure it out, then we're going to be in real trouble."],[1035.49,"Which amounts to, and a lot of the people who talk that way aren't even Calvinists."],[1040.77,"But it uh it amounts to kind of a really disturbing view of God."],[1045.73,"That God has something you need to do, or you're going to be face consequences."],[1050.13,"You're going to be punished for it."],[1051.25,"But He's not going to tell you."],[1053.73,"He's not just going to tell you."],[1055.57,"You have to figure it out."],[1059.17,"And that's that's simply a skewed view."],[1064.85,"The Bible, for example, the classic passage is Proverbs 31, tells a young man the kind of woman. he should be looking for to marry"],[1076.17,"Uh so if you find a woman who is like that, and you would like to marry her, that is a good choice to make."],[1091.69,"And if you make a mistake and don't find her, but another person, disaster is going to happen."],[1098.99,"Yeah. The reality is God gives us guidance and he gives us wisdom through the scriptures."],[1103.95,"But he hasn't encoded somebody's name in a Bible code."],[1107.07,"He hasn't I I remember a sermon about this from 40 years ago."],[1112.91,"So never despair."],[1114.35,"Sermons are can be remembered."],[1116.75,"Uh the the uh uh and the the priestess was in Episcopal Church said that when we we are thinking about you know which is the right choice of these two things God isn't really so much interested as to which we choose as what we do when we've chosen it and how we go about living that out."],[1140.07,"And uh you know assuming it's not between hatchet murder and there are things that the Bible clearly tells us."],[1149.27,"Yeah. Yeah. But between two jobs, what I think was the example he he was using, and whether to take this one or that one."],[1156.95,"But it it's more the way you you live after you've made that choice and what what you do with that choice than the choice itself."],[1166.61,"And and there may come a time in your life when you're older and you look back and you say, Well, maybe I shouldn't have married this person."],[1177.25,"And that's where God is going to be looking at what you do with that thought."],[1181.57,"Uh-huh. Yeah. Because some people will have that thought and follow it out and decide to go get divorced and try and find someone else."],[1189.89,"And some people will have that thought and say, you know what?"],[1195.33,"This is this is the person I married, and I'm going to love them, and I'm going to stay with them for the rest of my life."],[1200.55,"Um, you know, and the scriptures certainly tell us which one of those. is the one we should opt for"],[1209.31,"So, yes, um there's not uh God doesn't work through a magic eight ball, and that's certainly not what St."],[1215.95,"Paul is talking about here."],[1217.63,"That somehow there is some kind of mind transformation that will come about, and you'll make all the right choices in those things, whether to take the freeway or surface streets."],[1230.19,"You know, that's um, and you'll do it unerringly once you have this uh experience."],[1238.27,"Um but what what he is what he is talking about is uh the will of God is what God is doing in the world."],[1248.61,"The way the will is is understood by saint paul and was understood in the ancient world that everything that has a nature has a will so you'll find ancient writers like Aristotle talking about rocks having wills right the rock's will is to go down to the ground right um but you know a a tree has a will right that is causing it to grow into a tree and so things have wills based on their on their natures."],[1280.93,"And so the will of God is what is behind is the direction of the energies of God, of God's working in the world right and so Saint Paul is talking about look, we don't go and look at the world the way the people of the world look at the world."],[1300.29,"We don't judge the things to be valuable that they judge to be valuable."],[1304.21,"We don't judge the things to be important that they judge to be important."],[1310.85,"We need to be transformed so that when we look at the world, we look at the world the way God looks at the world."],[1315.97,"We see what God is doing in the world."],[1318.77,"And so for Saint Paul, once he saw what God was doing in the world and that he's doing it through Jesus Christ, all of a sudden his reputation as a rabbi is not important."],[1327.59,"is not important. And the acceptance of his peers in Jerusalem anymore"],[1335.67,"All of these things that he thought, you know, how advanced he is as a Pharisee, the tassels on his garments."],[1342.21,"All these things suddenly become become unimportant to him because he sees what God is doing and wants to become a part of it."],[1352.45,"This always causes me to have problems with okay, uh is God willing all the evil."],[1363.09,"And uh I I guess I don't need to explain more than that."],[1366.89,"Well, this is this is important because at its very root, at its very root, American culture, especially, is deeply Calvinist."],[1381.45,"That's certainly true."],[1382.17,"e. Is deply Calvinist"],[1385.33,"Even people who hate Calvinism are deeply Calvinists."],[1391.01,"Oh, that's me."],[1392.05,"And that's in that, in that uh they are essentially what are called monergists."],[1400.77,"Meaning there's only one, I was gonna say one person, but of course they believe in the Trinity."],[1407.33,"But only God is acting in the world."],[1409.49,"Uh-huh. Oh, yeah."],[1411.75,"Only God is acting."],[1413.51,"They will just Calvinists will describe themselves as monarchists."],[1416.95,"They mean it usually, sometimes they mean it over everything."],[1420.63,"They usually mainly focus on using that word in regard to salvation."],[1424.07,"Uh huh. Because from the classical Protestant and Calvinist perspective, in terms of our salvation, we do nothing."],[1426.77,"We contribute nothing, we do nothing"],[1433.29,"God does it to us."],[1436.49,"But that thinking is deeply within American culture about everything."],[1443.85,"Exhibit A, we just had the anniversary of 9 11."],[1448.57,"Remember after 9 11, people like Jerry Falwell hated Calvinism, but comes out and said, here's why God caused this to happen."],[1457.85,"Yeah. Uh-hhu. Because of these sins of the United States."],[1461.46,"So hates Calvinism, not a Calvinist, but God did 9 11."],[1468.66,"In his in his thinking."],[1470.26,"ing. And so because we have this think, we look at everything that happens in the world and we ascribe it to God"],[1477.58,"But this is not what Christianity has always taught."],[1480.94,"Certainly not what St."],[1481.9,"Paul teaches. We'll see a very pointed example of that in Philippians, where he says, we are, it's usually translated, we are God's fellow workers or co workers."],[1491.5,"It literally says synergoi, which is the opposite of monarchism."],[1495.9,"It's synergism. If you work against him, you're going to be destroyed."],[1505.9,"Yeah. You're gonna be destroyed in the process."],[1508.38,"But you could you can try and do that."],[1511.34,"And so the teaching of Christianity has always been that God is working in the world, but he's working in the world against spiritual forces of evil, and that human beings are in this position in our present age, in between the two."],[1534.34,"In between the two."],[1550.02,"And children of children of God."],[1552.26,"And most of the people and contrasts them."],[1554.87,"But the idea here is that we are going to be in this world agents of one or the other."],[1559.59,"We are going to serve one or the other"],[1565.79,"And we see this already."],[1568.27,"The reason in 1 John he starts with Cain is that if you read Second Temple Jewish literature about Cain, he's seen as exhibit A of this on the evil side."],[1580.77,"Through him, all this evil and murder and wickedness comes into the world."],[1585.89,"Through him and his descendants."],[1587.81,"It just sort of snowballs until you get to the flood."],[1590.85,"And God wipes the slate clean and starts over."],[1594.85,"But so that this is there are forces of evil who are seeking to destroy human beings."],[1600.75,"And destroy God's creation."],[1603.07,"Human beings are cooperating in doing that."],[1605.67,"And God is working in the world against that."],[1610.33,"Chiefly in the person of Jesus Christ."],[1613.13,"And so we we know from the scriptures, you know, if you go to the end of the Bible and you get to the book of Revelation, but even before that, in the Gospels, Chris, we know how this ends."],[1622.97,"Uh huh, yeah."],[1623.7,"This ends with the their destruction."],[1626.5,"And when it talks about a negative eternal consequence for humans, remember it always says they will go to the lake of fire prepared for the devil and his angels."],[1638.9,"Not for them."],[1639.78,"Uh-huh. But because they've chosen to take that side, you know, if that's who you want to be with, that's who you could be with."],[1647.04,"Um, but so this is St"],[1652.26,"Paul's understanding. So St."],[1655.86,"Paul is talking about being able to discern, and this is very personal for him because Christ came and died and rose again, and Saint Paul missed it."],[1665.54,"Yeah. Uh huh."],[1666.6,"He didn't see what God was doing."],[1668.92,"In fact, he thought that was not God doing it."],[1671.5,"He thought this was an evil thing that he needed to stop and stomp out of the world."],[1675.66,"So this is this is what he's talking about."],[1677.82,"We can go through life thinking the way the world thinks."],[1683.31,"And valuing things the way the world values them and miss what it is that God is doing in the world."],[1689.95,"Miss what he's doing and end up on the wrong side of what God is doing, the way he did"],[1696.45,"Or, as he was renewed in his mind, uh, we can we can by offering ourselves a sacrifice, by doing the things he said first, that's how we experience this renewing of the mind, we can come to see what God is doing in the world and then become a part of it."],[1713.75,"Yeah. Well, that's become a part of it."],[1715.99,"Very helpful the distinction between the monarchist idea, because I actually know better, I've heard this before, but I keep falling back into it out of out of habits."],[1726.65,"And everything that happens is God doing it."],[1729.39,"And no, sometimes we're doing it to ourselves."],[1732.11,"Yeah um sometimes it's the devil but God can act to bring good out of it right even the worst of it God can work act to bring like the crucifixion good out of it yes eventually so yes this is what it's about and being part of what God is doing in the world means right the energies of God are our love that he's pouring out into the world we can become the vehicle through which God shows his love to people."],[1766.12,"And in the process of doing that, we're transformed ourselves."],[1771.3,"And all of the things God is doing."],[1776.06,"God is working to make peace."],[1777.7,"God is working."],[1778.58,"All of these things are things that we receive ourselves by participating in God bringing them into bringing them into the world."],[1790.26,"But to do that, the first step is we've got to be able to perceive what it is that God is doing."],[1796.98,"And in order to do that, we have to make our lives holy and offer them to God so that we'll be able to discern that."],[1809.18,"For I say, through the grace given to me to everyone who is among you not to think of himself more highly than he ought to think, but to think soberly as God has dealt to each one a measure of faith."],[1825.34,"So a couple of things here."],[1826.7,"The first part seems kind of obvious."],[1829.5,"Don't think more of your think of yourself more highly than you ought to think, but to think soberly."],[1837.66,"As God has dealt to each one a measure of faith."],[1841.26,"Now remember the part I don't quite."],[1843.56,"Remember, faith is not just believe."],[1847.46,"Like you need to have more faith."],[1848.98,"What does that mean?"],[1849.7,"I need to believe yes harder."],[1852.26,"Yes, yes, harder."],[1854.58,"Yeah. No, that's not."],[1857.7,"But it's it's faithfulness."],[1861.28,"It's it's faithfulness, it's loyalty, it's trust, and it's faithfulness in actions."],[1867.89,"So when you're talking about a measure, a good illustration is the one that Christ used."],[1873.35,"Parable of the talents."],[1875.11,"Uh uh. The one servant is given ten, another servant is given five, another one is given one."],[1881.51,"Or is it it's five, two, and one."],[1883.91,"I doubled it."],[1884.87,"Anyway, five, two, and one."],[1886.71,"But so, yeah."],[1888.15,"So the first two servants both go and are both faithful with what they were given."],[1892.63,"Uh-huh. Even though they were given different amounts."],[1894.82,"And present different amounts."],[1896.24,"And the third one is unfaithful with what he was given."],[1901.18,"So he's not condemned for having a smaller amount."],[1904.78,"Uh-huh. It's that he didn't do anything with it."],[1910.07,"And so he's talking about our own self-evaluation."],[1917.69,"Our own self-evaluation."],[1920.09,"That's what he means by thinking soberly."],[1923.03,"Being honest in our judgment of ourselves."],[1925.99,"Rather than thinking of ourselves more highly."],[1928.79,"The opposite of that is not thinking poorly of yourself and denigrating yourself."],[1933.05,"Sort of a false show of human."],[1934.65,"Oh, I'm a lowly worm, you know, I'm worthless."],[1937.29,"But right, that's not what he's right."],[1939.61,"It's but it's being honest."],[1941.13,"Uh-huh. It's being honest."],[1942.55,"And according to that measure of faithfulness is being honest about what we've done with what we've been given."],[1950.16,"So we don't judge ourselves over against someone else."],[1954.5,"Well, this person is doing A, B, C, D, E, F, G."],[1960.82,"I've got, I've, you know, I've got nothing compared to that, so we think poorly of ourselves."],[1967.38,"Or conversely, this other person has been given less."],[1971.54,"We're doing a little more, and so we think, you know, we're doing pretty good, you know, rather than being honest."],[1981.0,"For as we have many members in one body, but all the members do not have the same function, so we being many are one body in Christ, and individually members of one another."],[1993.28,"Okay. So he makes this members here is referring literally to body parts."],[1999.22,"Right in this analogy."],[2000.58,"He says, so you've got your body has a whole bunch of different parts."],[2003.54,"They're not all the same."],[2004.82,"They're not all as useful."],[2006.5,"They're not all as important."],[2007.62,"Some of them you can add."],[2009.54,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2012.42,"You wouldn't want to get any of them removed, but there are some that would be worse than others."],[2016.18,"You could do better without a pinky than without a head."],[2018.68,"So um, but they all have their functions."],[2021.98,"He says, so in the same way, we're one body in Christ."],[2025.28,"So he's saying to the Romans, the whole community is one body."],[2029.3,"So you've been given these different gifts."],[2031.54,"Connecting this to the talents, the the pinky may be a guy that's been given less."],[2037.12,"Uh and the brain is one who's been given more, but uh each is each has the same responsibility essentially to to do its function properly and and productively."],[2052.09,"To offer their service, their service to God."],[2056.97,"Because if we're one we're one body in Christ."],[2059.79,"Remember Christ is acting in the world."],[2064.17,"How do we interface with the world and act in the world?"],[2066.65,"Through our bodies."],[2068.87,"So the pinky is called to do different things."],[2071.43,"Yeah. Then the nose."],[2074.09,"But each one has to be faithful. to its own task and its own purpose"],[2083.49,"Having then gifts differing according to the grace that is given to us."],[2088.13,"The grace here could literally means gift."],[2090.3,"The gifts that are given to us."],[2092.62,"Let us use them."],[2095.42,"Now you notice us use let us use them is not actually there."],[2101.34,"But it's conveying the idea."],[2103.98,"If prophecy, let us prophesy in proportion to our faith, or ministry, let us use it in our ministering."],[2111.26,"He who teaches and teaching, he who exhorts and exhortations, he who gives with liberality, he who leads with diligence, he who shows mercy with cheerfulness."],[2123.3,"So he gives more concrete."],[2125.94,"Remember, we're in the second half."],[2127.54,"So he gives more concrete examples."],[2129.88,"After using his analogy, he says, look, if you're the person who God has blessed with a lot of wealth, so that the way you can help the community is by giving, then you need to give generously."],[2144.38,"Because that's what's been given to you."],[2146.46,"If you're a person who's good at public speaking, that's exhortation."],[2152.62,"Then you need to go and preach, you know, and do that to the to the best of uh your ability."],[2158.7,"If you're a person who's good at helping those who are suffering or mourning, then you need to go and do that and do it with cheerfulness, not with despair."],[2169.15,"Falling into despair."],[2171.07,"So all of these things, are concrete examples of things that are going on in the community."],[2176.81,"And we could add piles and piles more."],[2183.43,"He says, Let love be without hypocrisy, abhor what is evil, cling to what is good."],[2190.71,"So that's fairly straightforward."],[2194.44,"Easy to say."],[2195.8,"Uh let love be without hypocrisy is pointing to something very human."],[2202.28,"Where, you know, because basic Christian truth is that we should love our brother, we should love everyone, we should love our neighbor, and everybody's our neighbor."],[2210.96,"And love even our enemies."],[2212.58,"It's very easy to say, oh yeah, I love everybody."],[2216.42,"And what we usually mean by that is I don't have anything right now against anybody that I'm willing to admit."],[2223.27,"And maybe maybe it is just that I don't have anything against anybody."],[2227.11,"I'm not holding any grudges."],[2228.79,"We very easily mistake just lack of caring."],[2232.71,"Oh yeah. Just sort of benign indifference for love."],[2237.03,"Uh huh. Which isn't what love is."],[2238.95,"No. Love is love is an active an active thing that requires actions from us."],[2247.39,"To express that love and to show that love."],[2250.19,"The marker isn't, would I say that I love everyone?"],[2253.75,"The marker is if you asked everyone I know, would they say that I loved them?"],[2259.67,"Yeah. Or would they say, what?"],[2264.55,"Would they say, I'm not sure?"],[2266.39,"Uh-huh. Would they say, well, what do you mean by love?"],[2273.43,"I mean, he's never done anything bad to me."],[2275.75,"That's not love."],[2276.79,"No. So this is what he means when he said love must be without hypocrisy."],[2281.19,"Not just, oh yeah, you say you love everybody."],[2284.95,"But it needs to be an active thing where you're showing love to each other within the community, and people are aware of it, you know, that you love them."],[2294.55,"Yeah. And of course, hate what is evil, cling to what is good"],[2300.35,"They use abhor because they don't want to use hate, but it really says hate."],[2304.51,"Uh-huh. Really says hate."],[2306.67,"There are things that we are allowed to and supposed to hate."],[2312.43,"Uh we're supposed to hate sin."],[2315.31,"Okay. We're supposed to hate evil."],[2318.11,"Um, those are not things that it is bad to hate."],[2321.87,"Yeah, see that I have trouble with that because I've gotten uh I've I've developed into a person who who really wants to love everybody and wants to, that is, and uh uh not harm anybody. and everything and so I I have trouble with the idea that there are things that I'm allowed to hate"],[2349.59,"Not people. Not people okay not people but things and this is important in terms of what it is to love people."],[2365.45,"Yeah the problem is when the person does the thing making the distinction."],[2375.47,"And I'm not so much talking about actions here."],[2378.11,"Uh-huh. I'm not making a distinctions between people and actions."],[2381.31,"I'm making a distinction between people and what might motivate their actions."],[2385.15,"Uh-huh. So falsehoods, false beliefs, ideologies, mental and spiritual sicknesses and illnesses."],[2399.81,"The devil. Things that motivate people to do the th the evil and wicked things they do."],[2411.98,"Those are the things we should hate because those are the things that are our enemies."],[2417.02,"And by our I mean human, humanities."],[2420.44,"They're my enemies."],[2421.72,"They're that person's enemies."],[2423.88,"The fact that this other person has fallen prey to that shouldn't cause me to hate the person."],[2430.96,"It's more like the person has a disease."],[2433.78,"This person has fallen victim."],[2435.87,"Is being victimized by this thing."],[2438.29,"And so what's right for me is to join this person in battle against that thing."],[2445.71,"Yeah. And this is part of."],[2449.97,"This is this is part of."],[2452.55,"And we talk about how the church is a hospital for sinners."],[2457.56,"This is what we mean."],[2458.52,"We're here fighting disease."],[2461.18,"That's in that's in all of us."],[2463.26,"But going back to Cain again, when Cain is first jealous of his brother, God comes to him and says, sin is crouching at your door."],[2471.48,"Like it's a lion or a predatory animal."],[2474.2,"You know, it wants to master you, but you must master it."],[2477.64,"You need to struggle with it, and you need to win."],[2479.96,"And he doesn't win, he loses."],[2482.94,"He loses when he gives it."],[2485.66,"But that sin was both Cain and Abel's enemy, and Adam and Eve, and Seth, and and everyone else."],[2494.76,"So there's a basic truth here that St."],[2498.92,"Paul is getting at."],[2501.29,"When we're looking at another person and we hate what is evil and we cling to what is good."],[2506.35,"Which is them, which is the person God created, that we hold on to."],[2511.19,"That no human being is my enemy"],[2516.65,"No human being is my enemy."],[2518.73,"All of us as human beings are on the same side."],[2521.93,"We all have enemies who are out to destroy us."],[2525.21,"We all have a God who loves us."],[2528.97,"We all have a Lord Jesus Christ who gave himself for us."],[2532.82,"So we're all on the same side, whether we know it or not."],[2536.58,"But being a canny foe, our enemies are very good at turning us on each other."],[2543.7,"Because a house divided against itself can't stand."],[2547.44,"Turning human beings on each other."],[2549.84,"But part of loving another person is realizing that solidarity."],[2556.68,"Realizing that we all share the same human nature and we all share it with Christ."],[2561.82,"And so you're a fellow soldier in the same battle."],[2566.62,"If you get shot down, and you fall and your gun goes off and hits me, that doesn't make you the enemy."],[2573.87,"Yeah. That makes you someone I need to help more."],[2578.38,"In the struggle."],[2580.14,"And so this is this is very difficult for us."],[2583.82,"It runs against again the world's understanding."],[2588.28,"The world wants to tell us there's good people and there's bad people."],[2592.52,"And somehow everyone who says that is one of the good people."],[2596.18,"No one. has ever said that and then identified themselves as one of the bad people"],[2601.34,"And it it seems to be just built into our worldview that we separate everything into groups."],[2608.3,"You know, it's red versus blue, our tribe versus their tribe."],[2612.24,"Our country versus their country."],[2614.8,"Our versus them."],[2616.56,"I teach that in school, but um nationalism uh sort of all come tends to come up."],[2627.68,"That it isn't natural for one nation to hate another."],[2631.2,"It is natural for us to identify us versus them, and that can be men versus women, uh seniors versus freshmen, uh you know, our team against your team, and yeah, it changes our political party, your political party."],[2648.52,"It changes moment by moment depending on what we're thinking of, but we always have this us them."],[2653.86,"Where we're we're pitted against against other people."],[2658.82,"Yeah, and and we aren't really right, and so that that needs to be basic to us loving our neighbor, is breaking that down."],[2670.66,"Um coming to accept that another person, even though they're different from us, is not another thing different from me, but it's another self like me."],[2681.38,"This is a person like me who has thoughts and hopes and experiences like I do."],[2687.74,"They're maybe different from mine, but they have them."],[2691.98,"And so this is a person who is precious to God also."],[2695.5,"Why why is this so important for St."],[2697.9,"Paul to bring up now?"],[2698.94,"Well, remember the problem here."],[2702.14,"You've got Jewish believers and Gentile believers, and who are thinking and who are thinking of themselves as in terms of their differences."],[2712.08,"Here's the way they're not like me."],[2715.17,"And so love has to break that down."],[2719.03,"That we're all one."],[2721.11,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2727.67,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"],[2733.79,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":30.0,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: two halves of Paul's epistles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Romans 12:1</b>; last time ended at a <b>major transition point</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul structures all epistles in <b>two parts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">part 1: <b>theological groundwork</b>, lots of Old Testament</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>\"therefore\"</b> → move to <b>practical application</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The \"therefore\" does two things</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>marks the transition</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>connects</b> part 2 to part 1 - basis for second = the first</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: did he choose part-1 themes in light of what he addresses? - <b>\"Exactly\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":109.82,"lv":3,"h":"Romans so far","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Part 1 = relationship of <b>Jewish and Gentile believers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">issues from <b>Claudius expelling the Jews</b> from Rome, then their return</li><li class=\"d0\">Handled at theological level</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>patriarchal narratives</b> in Genesis: Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Esau, Ishmael</li><li class=\"d1\">clear <b>pattern</b> in how God deals with his people</li><li class=\"d0\">Pattern continues in <b>new covenant</b>, <b>fulfilled in Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same pattern <b>writ larger</b> - not just Judea, <b>all nations</b> in one plan</li><li class=\"d0\">Now: what this means for <b>Christian community in Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how you <b>live, worship, be a community</b> together in shared confession of Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":222.46,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 12:1 - Present your bodies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice, wholly acceptable to God, which is your reasonable service\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Very King Jamesy\" even in NKJV</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>therefore</b> moved in word order - \"I beseech you, therefore\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= based on everything I've just said</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Reasonable service\"</b> = oddest translation</li><li class=\"d1\">means <b>more than logical</b></li></ul>"},{"t":286.2,"lv":3,"h":"Body is not flesh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">He says <b>body</b> - watch Paul's <b>word choice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Jews and Israel</b> not the same in Paul</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> = mostly the <b>sinful aspect</b> of our now mortal nature</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Body</b> = our <b>physicality</b>, life in the <b>material world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">includes our <b>interaction with matter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for Paul <b>not negative</b> - flesh negative, body not</li><li class=\"d0\">Missing the distinction → <b>Gnostic reading</b> of Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">flesh = meat → <b>soul/rational = good, material = bad</b></li><li class=\"d1\">basic feature of all Gnostic systems - <b>not Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Clearer in <b>1 Corinthians</b>: body now vs body in resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>same body</b>, transformed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>flesh goes away, physicality does not</b></li></ul>"},{"t":426.97,"lv":3,"h":"Living sacrifice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Presenting <b>bodies, physicality, life in the world</b> as living sacrifice</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>suicide or human sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Living</b> = <b>ongoing</b>, not one-time act</li><li class=\"d1\">normal sacrifice = one-time, consumed</li><li class=\"d1\">ongoing offering of our <b>material life</b> to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Echo of the litany: <b>\"commend ourselves and each other and our whole life unto Christ our God\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>10%</b> of life - <b>whole life and self in this world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":520.46,"lv":2,"h":"Holy and pure; rational worship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Acceptable sacrifice must be <b>holy, pure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT: animal <b>without blemish</b>, no broken leg - <b>your best</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If offering ourselves on an ongoing basis → keep ourselves and lives <b>holy and pure</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two ideas go together</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Reasonable service\"</b> = <b>rational worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">brings in the <b>immaterial</b> - soul, mind</li><li class=\"d1\">worship God with mind and soul, <b>not apart from the body</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Our own \"Gnosticism\": <b>separation of church and state</b>, worship as <b>occasional</b>, disconnected from rest of life</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>ties them together - inseparable</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Eucharist, liturgy, services - but worship = <b>whole life at every point</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not offering part, the whole thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Romans' version: soul/mind worship separate from daily life; ours: <b>segmented lives</b></li></ul>"},{"t":713.51,"lv":2,"h":"Q: Protestant rejection of the body","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: raised <b>Presbyterian</b> - religion = getting rid of <b>physicality</b>, living immaterial life</li><li class=\"d1\">morality = <b>abstinence</b> from worldly things</li><li class=\"d1\">\"body dies, not important, <b>the soul lives</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Even Protestants who deny this: worship framed that way</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>iconoclastic</b>, plain space, attention in one place</li><li class=\"d1\">sit quietly, <b>intake information intellectually</b>, <b>highly verbal</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Segmentation</b> - offering yourself is <b>active</b>, not passive abstaining</li><li class=\"d0\">Calvinism: <b>\"negative asceticism\"</b> - don't drink, dance, play cards</li><li class=\"d1\">giving <b>time, wealth, attention</b> to needy as sacrifice has <b>fallen out</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fear of <b>works righteousness</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pelagius</b> condemned long before Calvin</li><li class=\"d0\">Arrogance of \"I'm really holy\" <b>just as inherent in what I don't do</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pharisee</b> by bragging about what you don't do</li></ul>"},{"t":918.77,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 12:2 - Transformed by the renewing of the mind","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, that you may prove what is that good and acceptable and perfect will of God\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now the <b>mind</b> - behavior already covered by living sacrifice and holiness</li><li class=\"d1\">how the mind <b>thinks and values things</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prove</b> = <b>testing</b>, like proving a metal is pure</li><li class=\"d1\">so you can <b>discern</b></li></ul>"},{"t":988.54,"lv":3,"h":"Discerning the will of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">American Christian use: <b>whom to marry, what major, which job</b></li><li class=\"d1\">assumes God already made the choice; wrong choice = <b>disaster</b></li><li class=\"d1\">have to <b>\"voodoo\"</b> what God wants - <b>disturbing view of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even non-Calvinists talk this way; \"simply a skewed view\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Proverbs 31</b> - tells a young man what kind of woman to look for</li><li class=\"d1\">find one like that and want to marry her = <b>good choice</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God gives <b>guidance and wisdom through Scripture</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no name encoded in a Bible code</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Story: Episcopal sermon 40 yrs ago - God cares less <b>which</b> you choose than <b>what you do</b> once chosen</li><li class=\"d1\">later \"maybe I shouldn't have married this person\" → what you <b>do with that thought</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stay and love, or divorce - Scripture tells which to opt for</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a <b>\"magic eight ball\"</b> - not freeway vs surface streets</li></ul>"},{"t":1238.27,"lv":3,"h":"The will of God = what God is doing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Will of God</b> = <b>what God is doing in the world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient view: <b>everything with a nature has a will</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b>: a rock's will is to go <b>down to the ground</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tree's will → grows into a tree</li><li class=\"d0\">God's will = direction of the <b>energies of God</b>, his working in the world</li><li class=\"d0\">Don't look at the world as the world does</li><li class=\"d1\">don't judge <b>valuable / important</b> what they do</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>transformed</b> → see the world <b>the way God sees it</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul's own case</b>: once he saw what God was doing through Jesus</li><li class=\"d1\">reputation as <b>rabbi</b>, acceptance of <b>peers in Jerusalem</b> - unimportant</li><li class=\"d1\">how advanced a <b>Pharisee</b>, tassels on garments - unimportant</li><li class=\"d1\">wants to <b>become part of it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1352.45,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Is God willing all the evil? Monergism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: problem - <b>is God willing all the evil?</b></li><li class=\"d0\">American culture <b>deeply Calvinist</b> - even those who hate Calvinism</li><li class=\"d1\">essentially <b>monergists</b>: <b>only God is acting</b></li><li class=\"d1\">mainly about <b>salvation</b>: we do nothing, contribute nothing, <b>God does it to us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but thinking spread to <b>everything</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Exhibit A: <b>9/11</b> anniversary</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jerry Falwell</b> (hates Calvinism): \"God caused this\" for the <b>sins of the US</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ascribe everything that happens to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Not what Christianity taught, <b>not Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Philippians</b>: \"co-workers\" = literally <b><i>synergoi</i></b></li><li class=\"d1\">opposite of monergism = <b>synergism</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1477.58,"lv":2,"h":"God against spiritual forces; two sides","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God works in the world <b>against spiritual forces of evil</b></li><li class=\"d1\">humans in present age <b>in between the two</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we are <b>agents of one or the other</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 John</b> starts with <b>Cain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Second Temple literature: Cain = <b>exhibit A on the evil side</b></li><li class=\"d1\">evil and murder <b>snowball</b> through him and descendants → <b>flood</b>, God wipes slate clean</li><li class=\"d0\">Evil forces seek to <b>destroy humans and creation</b>; humans cooperate</li><li class=\"d1\">God works against it, <b>chiefly in Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>We know how it ends</b> - Revelation, Gospels: <b>their destruction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lake of fire <b>\"prepared for the devil and his angels\"</b> - not for humans</li><li class=\"d1\">humans choose that side: \"if that's who you want to be with\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Humans who end there <b>chose to take that side</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1652.26,"lv":2,"h":"Paul missed it; seeing what God is doing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Very <b>personal for Paul</b>: Christ died and rose, <b>Paul missed it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">thought it was <b>not God</b> - an evil thing to <b>stomp out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We can think the way the world thinks, <b>value as it values</b>, <b>miss what God is doing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">end up <b>on the wrong side</b>, like Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Renewed mind: <b>offer ourselves as sacrifice first</b> → that's how we experience the <b>renewing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then see what God is doing and <b>become part of it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: helpful distinction from monergism - habit of \"everything is <b>God doing it</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes <b>we do it to ourselves</b>; sometimes <b>the devil</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God can <b>bring good out of even the worst</b> - the <b>crucifixion</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Being part of it: <b>energies of God = his love</b> poured out</li><li class=\"d1\">we become the <b>vehicle</b> of God's love to people → <b>we're transformed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we <b>receive</b> peace etc. by participating in bringing them into the world</li><li class=\"d0\">First step: <b>perceive what God is doing</b> → make lives holy, offer them</li></ul>"},{"t":1809.18,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 12:3 - Think soberly","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Not to think of himself more highly than he ought to think, but to think soberly, as God has dealt to each one a measure of faith\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith</b> not just \"believe\" - not \"believe <b>harder</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>faithfulness, loyalty, trust</b>, faithfulness <b>in actions</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Measure</b> - <b>parable of the talents</b></li><li class=\"d1\">five, two, one</li><li class=\"d1\">first two <b>both faithful</b> with different amounts</li><li class=\"d1\">third <b>unfaithful</b> - condemned not for smaller amount but <b>did nothing with it</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Thinking soberly</b> = honest <b>self-evaluation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">opposite is <b>not</b> \"I'm a lowly worm\" - a <b>false show of humility</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>honest about what we've done with what we've been given</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Don't judge ourselves <b>against someone else</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they do A through G, I've got nothing\" <b>or</b> \"they have less, I'm doing pretty good\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1981.0,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 12:4-5 - One body, many members","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Many members in one body, but not all the same function... one body in Christ, individually members of one another\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Members</b> = literally <b>body parts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not all same, not all as <b>useful or important</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"could do better without a <b>pinky</b> than without a <b>head</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all have <b>functions</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Whole community of Romans = <b>one body</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pinky = one given less; brain = one given more</li><li class=\"d1\">same responsibility: do its function <b>properly and productively</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ is acting in the world</b> - we interface with the world <b>through our bodies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pinky does different things than the <b>nose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">each <b>faithful to its own task and purpose</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2083.49,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 12:6-8 - Differing gifts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Having then gifts differing according to the grace given to us\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>grace</b> here could literally mean <b>gift</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"let us use them\" <b>not actually in the text</b> but conveys the idea</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophecy, ministry, teaching, exhortation, giving, leading, mercy</li><li class=\"d0\">Second half → <b>more concrete examples</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Wealth</b> → give <b>generously</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Good at <b>public speaking</b> = exhortation → <b>preach</b>, do it to the best of your ability</li><li class=\"d0\">Good at helping those <b>suffering or mourning</b> → do it <b>with cheerfulness</b>, not despair</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Piles and piles more\" examples could be added</li></ul>"},{"t":2183.43,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 12:9 - Love without hypocrisy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Let love be without hypocrisy. Abhor what is evil, cling to what is good\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Easy to say <b>\"I love everybody\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">usually means <b>no grudge I'm willing to admit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we mistake <b>lack of caring</b>, \"benign indifference,\" for love</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Love is active</b> - requires <b>actions</b> to express and show it</li><li class=\"d0\">The marker: <b>would everyone I know say I loved them?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">or \"what do you mean by love?\" - \"he's never done anything bad to me\" - <b>\"That's not love\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Without hypocrisy</b> = shown <b>within the community</b>, people <b>aware</b> you love them</li></ul>"},{"t":2294.55,"lv":3,"h":"Hate what is evil - people vs things","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Abhor</b> is used because they don't want to say hate - <b>really says hate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">supposed to <b>hate sin, hate evil</b> - not bad to hate those</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: trouble - wants to love everybody, <b>not harm anybody</b>, trouble with things allowed to hate</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just actions vs people - <b>what motivates actions</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>falsehoods, false beliefs, ideologies, mental and spiritual sicknesses, the devil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">These are <b>our enemies</b> - mine and that person's</li><li class=\"d1\">person fallen prey is like <b>a person with a disease</b>, victim of the thing</li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>join him in battle</b> against it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Church = hospital for sinners</b> - fighting disease in all of us</li></ul>"},{"t":2463.26,"lv":3,"h":"Cain: no human being is my enemy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cain</b> jealous of Abel → God: <b>sin is crouching at your door</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like a <b>lion</b>; wants to master you, <b>you must master it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Cain <b>loses</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sin was enemy of <b>Cain, Abel, Adam, Eve, Seth</b> - everyone</li><li class=\"d0\">Hate what is evil, <b>cling to what is good</b> = the <b>person God created</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no human being is my enemy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all on <b>the same side</b> - enemies out to destroy us, <b>God who loves us</b>, <b>Christ who gave himself</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Our enemies are <b>canny</b> - good at <b>turning us on each other</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"house divided against itself can't stand\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Love = realizing <b>solidarity</b>: same human nature, shared with Christ</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fellow soldier</b>: if you fall and your gun goes off and hits me - <b>not the enemy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"someone I need to <b>help more</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2583.82,"lv":2,"h":"Us versus them; Jew and Gentile","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Against the world's view: <b>good people vs bad people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everyone who says it is <b>one of the good people</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Built into worldview: groups - <b>red vs blue</b>, tribe, country</li><li class=\"d1\">teacher: <b>not natural for one nation to hate another</b> - <b>natural to identify us vs them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">men/women, seniors/freshmen, teams, parties - <b>changes moment by moment</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Basic to loving neighbor: <b>break it down</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other person is <b>\"another self like me\"</b> - thoughts, hopes, experiences</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>precious to God also</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why bring it up now? <b>Jewish believers and Gentile believers</b> thinking in terms of <b>differences</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"here's the way they're not like me\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>love has to break that down - we're all one</b></li></ul>"}]});
